[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Mistrol gave away its content safety model this morning. 3 billion parameters, Apache 2.0. Free to download, free to run, free to modify. It fits on a single 16 GB graphics card. And on Mistl\'s own benchmarks, it ties a'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model nearly 7 times its size. That\'s the headline. It\'s also the least interesting thing in the release because what Shieldstro actually changes is who writes the safety rules, not the lab, you. And that shift comes with a bill'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'almost nobody has costed yet. Here\'s how safety models have worked until now. You take a model like Meta Lamagard and the harm categories are baked into the weights. Violence, self harm, sexual content, hate speech, a fixed list'

[00:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'decided by whoever trained it. If your product needs a different line drawn, you retrain. Weeks of work and a machine learning team you probably don\'t have. The paper puts the problem in one clean'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'example. The same content can be perfectly appropriate inside a cyber security research tool and deeply harmful on a mental health support platform. A fixed category model returns the identical label either way because it has no idea who is asking or why.'

[01:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Shield Straw\'s answer write the policy as a plain English question at the moment you ask it. No retraining. One correction, the coverage will get wrong today. This is not the first policy adaptive guard model. OpenAI shipped GPT'

[01:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'OSS safeguard last year at 20 billion parameters and Nvidia has Nemetron 3.5 safety, both marked adaptive. What\'s new is that the idea now fits on a gaming'

[01:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'graphics card under a license that lets you do whatever you want with it. And that should sound familiar to anyone who has worked in security. 25 years ago, we sold organizations firewalls and called the job done. The firewall was never the'

[01:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'security the rule set was. And rule sets drift because a text file with no owner always drifts. Temporary any any rules that sat in production for four years, nobody signed them, nobody reviewed'

[01:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them, and the auditor found them before we did. Shieldstroke is a firewall for content. The rule set just moved into a sentence. Here\'s what\'s happening underneath, and the shape of it matters more than the model does. Picture your'

[02:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'application as two layers. The bottom layer is the one everybody draws. A user sends a prompt. Before that prompt reaches your language model, it passes a gate. The model answers. Before that answer reaches the user, it passes a'

[02:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'second gate. Same checkpoint at both ends. This is one three billion parameter model doing both jobs, not two systems. Call that the data plane. It runs on every single request. Each gate returns one number between zero and one.'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Not a label, not a category, a score. Now the layer above it and this is the one that never makes it into the architecture slide sitting on top of both gates uh are three things. An instruction that sets the context and'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how strict to be a query the actual yes or no question you want answered and a threshold the cutoff above which a score becomes a blog. Three text fields and a decimal. Look at where the arrows come from. Every arrow points down. The gates'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the data plane don\'t decide anything. They compute. The deciding happens in the layer above and that layer is a configuration file. In network security, we\'ve had a name for this split for two'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'decades. Data plane, control plane. The traffic moves through one and the policy lives in the other. And every serious outage postmortem I have ever read traces back to the control plane, not'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the data plane. The packets were fine. Somebody changed the rule. This is that split arriving in AI safety at a price of zero. So, how does a question become a verdict? Every request ShieldStraw'

[03:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'receives has a fixed system message and three tagged fields. The system message never changes and it\'s worth hearing in full because it\'s the whole design in one line. Quote, judge whether the'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'document meets the requirements based on the query and the instruction provided. Note that the answer can only be yes or no. That\'s it. That\'s the meta task. Then the three fields, the instruct'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'field carries the framing, what you\'re evaluating and how strict to be. Mistral trained three registers deliberately. Strict for adversarial jailbreak content, moderate for general safety and hate speech, lenient for response'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quality. The query field is one yes or no question. Does this content promote violence? That sentence is your entire policy for that check. The document field is the thing being judged. A user'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompt, a model response, a prompt and response together, or an image with or without text. Same interface for all four. Then one forward pass. And here\'s the part that makes it fast. At'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inference, the model doesn\'t generate an answer. It reads out exactly two numbers. The log for the token yes and the log for the token no. Normalizes them and returns the result. Default cutoff 0.5. one token, no reasoning'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'trace, no explanation, no paragraph of justification. Compare that to GPTO OSS safeguard, which writes out a reasoning chain before it answers more accurate on'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some tasks and dramatically slower on every request you actually ship. And because the output is a score rather than a label, you can rank by confidence, autoblock the obvious cases, and route the uncertain middle to a'

[05:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'human. That single design choice is why this slots into a review workflow instead of replacing one. The model is a three billion parameter minestral 3B with a pixel vision encoder bolted on for images. Small, ordinary, nothing'

[05:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exotic. The interesting work is entirely in the data. 54.1 million training samples and how they were assembled. 45.2 2 million came from existing open safety data sets, which is the hard part'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because those data sets disagree with each other. Different taxonomies, different label formats, some binary, safe or unsafe, some fine grained multil wrote a processor for each one to'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'convert it into the same instruction query document shape, then randomized the phrasing so the model learns the task rather than the wording. Then the 4.4 4 million synthetic samples. And this is the clever bit. If you train a'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'safety model on a fixed set of policy labels, it memorizes the labels. Show it a policy it has never seen and it flounders. So instead, they built a hierarchy, 11 top level classes, 73 leaf categories, and for each one handed a'

[06:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'language model, a piece of safe text, a target category, and a sibling category. The model rewrites the text so it violates the target and not the sibling. Same text, two questions, opposite'

[06:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'answers. The model isn\'t learning this is unsafe. It\'s learning where the boundary between two near identical rules sits. That\'s the skill that transfers to a policy you invent next Tuesday. They trained two versions with'

[06:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Laura. One on the public data and one on public plus generated and merged them together with a slice of the plain instruction following base model. Base ministrol no safety training an F1 of zero. Add the public data 61.1.'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Add the generated contrastive data 84.4. Merge the checkpoints 88.7. Hold on to that first number. We\'re coming back to it. 16 benchmarks, 21'

[07:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'splits, 10 competing models. And before any of these numbers land, every one of them is Mistl\'s own evaluation on Mistl\'s own heldout splits published in Mistl\'s own paper. Read them as claims, not findings. On tech safety,'

